From 91f5ef139a2654984815df91f7233a707e354c0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 23:45:30 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] VideoOutput: 12 to 16 bit video reaches the compositor at 16
 bits; the layer allocates DMA-BUF buffers where the driver has no window
 surface of the depth

Content above 10 bits (12 bit too: half float holds about 11 significant bits) goes to the compositor in 16 bit unsigned normalised buffers, never capped.
StereoSurface16Bit is the new surface and what StereoSurfaceBySource gives above 10 bits (the passes make Float32 images from 12 bits up). The layer takes,
in this order:

 1. a window surface of the driver in the format (EGL_COLOR_COMPONENT_TYPE_FIXED_EXT, 16.16.16.16);
 2. buffers the layer allocates itself where the driver has none (NVIDIA lists 16 bit only as float): GBM on the render node of the EGL device
    (EGL_EXT_device_drm_render_node; Mesa, and nvidia-drm's GBM backend), imported as EGLImages, drawn into by the same pass, and given to the compositor
    with zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1 (create_immed) in a format and a modifier both the driver (rendering, not sampling only) and the compositor's feedback list
    (version 4 default feedback, tranche by tranche, ours first; the modifier events before); 10 bit content takes ABGR2101010 from the compositor's
    list before a half float window surface;
 3. wl_shm XBGR16161616, copied by the presenter, counted and said (layerPath, layerSurfaceFormat and the reason in the route).

QT_MM_STEREO_LAYER_DMABUF=1 draws every depth into the layer's own buffers (a control), 0 never. The route says what was observed: layerDmabuf is true when
frames were committed with the layer's own DMA-BUF buffers, layerDmabufOffered what the compositor offers, layerPath the route, layerBuffer the format and
modifier. A buffer pass with every buffer held by the compositor waits for a release instead of copying another way.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 via Claude Code
Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
